{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788369539116,"sessionID":"ses_f9cdf1744ffeXefkzNuE7Vt14g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8447fd6a8ef37595","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show --stat f22a806"},"output":"commit f22a8060efeb4cc81b8722763ee401b0943bca2e\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 10:08:40 2026 +0200\n\n    Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n    \n    A decimal-point literal is a Float: a separate type from Int with no\n    implicit conversion, so mixing the two in one operator is an error the\n    same way mixing Int and Int64 is. Division is total -- a zero divisor\n    returns Infinity, the IEEE answer, where Int stops the program -- and\n    NaN and Infinity print as their names. A Float reads off the wire as\n    the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\n    JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\n    \n    The other six backends get unreachable arms for Float nodes and types:\n    their exhaustive matches over the shared Type/Kind enums have to\n    compile, and Float never reaches them while it is JS-only. Their real\n    implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\n    \n    Derived: binary64 representation and total division (ADR 0007, the Q37\n    ruling), Float-in-input reading a JSON number, the separate-type and\n    no-implicit-conversion rules (Int64's precedent)\n    Agent-invented: float literal grammar (. and exponent only join when\n    digits follow), the shortest-round-trip literal spelling in src/float.rs\n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash <deepseek-v4-flash>\n\n build.rs               |   8 ++++\n src/ast.rs             |   8 ++++\n src/check/linearity.rs |   4 +-\n src/check/mod.rs       |  20 +++++++---\n src/emit_go.rs         |   6 +++\n src/emit_jq.rs         |   4 ++\n src/emit_js.rs         |  23 +++++++++--\n src/emit_llvm.rs       |   6 +++\n src/emit_lua.rs        |   3 ++\n src/emit_py.rs         |   2 +\n src/emit_rs.rs         |   5 +++\n src/emit_toylang.rs    |   1 +\n src/float.rs           |  16 ++++++++\n src/input.rs           |   6 +++\n src/lib.rs             |   1 +\n src/parse.rs           | 104 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------\n src/tags.rs            |   2 +\n src/tir.rs             |   5 +++\n src/ty.rs              |  14 ++++++-\n tests/backend_js.rs    |  44 +++++++++++++++++++++\n 20 files changed, 258 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)\n","metadata":{"output":"commit f22a8060efeb4cc81b8722763ee401b0943bca2e\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 10:08:40 2026 +0200\n\n    Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n    \n    A decimal-point literal is a Float: a separate type from Int with no\n    implicit conversion, so mixing the two in one operator is an error the\n    same way mixing Int and Int64 is. Division is total -- a zero divisor\n    returns Infinity, the IEEE answer, where Int stops the program -- and\n    NaN and Infinity print as their names. A Float reads off the wire as\n    the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\n    JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\n    \n    The other six backends get unreachable arms for Float nodes and types:\n    their exhaustive matches over the shared Type/Kind enums have to\n    compile, and Float never reaches them while it is JS-only. Their real\n    implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\n    \n    Derived: binary64 representation and total division (ADR 0007, the Q37\n    ruling), Float-in-input reading a JSON number, the separate-type and\n    no-implicit-conversion rules (Int64's precedent)\n    Agent-invented: float literal grammar (. and exponent only join when\n    digits follow), the shortest-round-trip literal spelling in src/float.rs\n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash <deepseek-v4-flash>\n\n build.rs               |   8 ++++\n src/ast.rs             |   8 ++++\n src/check/linearity.rs |   4 +-\n src/check/mod.rs       |  20 +++++++---\n src/emit_go.rs         |   6 +++\n src/emit_jq.rs         |   4 ++\n src/emit_js.rs         |  23 +++++++++--\n src/emit_llvm.rs       |   6 +++\n src/emit_lua.rs        |   3 ++\n src/emit_py.rs         |   2 +\n src/emit_rs.rs         |   5 +++\n src/emit_toylang.rs    |   1 +\n src/float.rs           |  16 ++++++++\n src/input.rs           |   6 +++\n src/lib.rs             |   1 +\n src/parse.rs           | 104 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------\n src/tags.rs            |   2 +\n src/tir.rs             |   5 +++\n src/ty.rs              |  14 ++++++-\n tests/backend_js.rs    |  44 +++++++++++++++++++++\n 20 files changed, 258 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show --stat f22a806","time":{"start":1788369539103,"end":1788369539110}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The issue is #149 with 0 comments. Good. Now I understand the issue. The JS implementation landed already (commit f22a806). Let me look at that JS commit to understand scope and shape, since the task says to follow float-build's JS implementation as the reference.\n\nLet me look at the JS implementation commit f22a806 and the current state of the repo.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063218dfe0010oNbsAZtYe2Eb7","sessionID":"ses_f9cdf1744ffeXefkzNuE7Vt14g","messageID":"msg_0632182df001ySu0RqZLPpTj4i"}}
