{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788369635491,"sessionID":"ses_f9cdf1744ffeXefkzNuE7Vt14g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a4424c4ad80bfab0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-rust/justfile"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-rust/justfile</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # List available recipes.\n2: default:\n3:     @just --list\n4: \n5: # The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\n6: test:\n7:     cargo nextest run\n8: \n9: # The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\n10: check:\n11:     cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n12: \n13: # Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n14: # `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\n15: euler-data DIR:\n16:     EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n17: \n18: # Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n19: # backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n20: # this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\n21: slow-test:\n22:     TOYLANG_SLOW=1 cargo nextest run\n23: \n24: # Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\n25: clippy:\n26:     cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n27: \n28: # Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\n29: fmt:\n30:     cargo run -q -- fmt\n31: \n32: # Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\n33: fmt-write:\n34:     cargo run -q -- fmt --write\n35: \n36: # The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\n37: checks:\n38:     bash .claude/checks/run.sh\n39: \n40: # The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\n41: drive:\n42:     .claude/scripts/drive-loop.sh\n43: \n44: # Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\n45: tick:\n46:     .claude/scripts/drive-tick.sh\n47: \n48: # Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\n49: peek:\n50:     .claude/scripts/tick-peek.sh\n\n(End of file - total 50 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# List available recipes.\ndefault:\n    @just --list\n\n# The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\ntest:\n    cargo nextest run\n\n# The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n\n# Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n# `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\neuler-data DIR:\n    EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n\n# Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n# backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n# this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-rust/justfile","text":"# List available recipes.\ndefault:\n    @just --list\n\n# The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\ntest:\n    cargo nextest run\n\n# The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n\n# Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n# `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\neuler-data DIR:\n    EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n\n# Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n# backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n# this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\nslow-test:\n    TOYLANG_SLOW=1 cargo nextest run\n\n# Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\nclippy:\n    cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n\n# Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\nfmt:\n    cargo run -q -- fmt\n\n# Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\nfmt-write:\n    cargo run -q -- fmt --write\n\n# The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\nchecks:\n    bash .claude/checks/run.sh\n\n# The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\ndrive:\n    .claude/scripts/drive-loop.sh\n\n# Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\ntick:\n    .claude/scripts/drive-tick.sh\n\n# Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\npeek:\n    .claude/scripts/tick-peek.sh","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":50,"truncated":false}},"title":"justfile","time":{"start":1788369635481,"end":1788369635490}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the shared infrastructure is in place. My task: implement the Rust backend's Float support in src/emit_rs.rs. Let me look at the four places that have unreachable for Float, and figure out what each needs.\n\n1. `rs_type`: `Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` -> should be `\"f64\".to_string()`\n2. `parser_expr`: `Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` -> needs `tl_parse_f64` parser (reading a JSON number off the wire)\n3. `expr`: `Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` -> needs `crate::float::lit(*n)` producing the literal\n4. `show`: `Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` -> needs to print. In Rust, `f64`'s Display prints the shortest round-trip decimal (like JS String()). But for non-finite values, Rust's Display prints `NaN`, `inf`, `-inf` — which does NOT match \"NaN\"/\"Infinity\"/\"-Infinity\" required. So we need custom printing.\n\nAlso:\n5. `arith`: the current `arith` function handles Div/Rem/Add/Sub/Mul with wrapping. For Float, division by zero should return Infinity (not trap). Rust's `f64::div` returns Infinity for `1.0 / 0.0` already. And `f64` doesn't have `wrapping_*`. So the `arith` function needs a Float arm.\n\nLet me think about the details of each:\n\n### 1. rs_type\n```rust\nType::Float => \"f64\".to_string(),\n```\n\n### 2. parser_expr\nThe JS backend reads input via `JSON.parse` (a JSON number is already a double). For the Rust backend, there's a hand-written parser (`TlParser`). It has `tl_parse_i32` that refuses non-integer numbers (checks for `.` or `e`/`E`). I need `tl_parse_f64` that parses a JSON number (integer or float, with sign, digits, decimal point, exponent) into an `f64`.\n\nLet me look at how the JS commit handled Int64 input refusal vs Float input acceptance. From the commit message: \"A Float reads off the wire as the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\" So the checker allows Float in input types, but refuses Int64. The `input.rs` validate allows `(Type::Float, Value::Number(_))`.\n\nBut also note the `check/mod.rs` — does the checker allow Float in input types? Let me check. The Int64 was refused because its wire codec is undecided. For Float, the parser needs to read JSON numbers. Let me look at how the check refuses Int64 in input, to see if Float is allowed. Let me search.\n\n### 3. expr (Kind::Float)\n```rust\nKind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n),\n```\nSame as JS.\n\n### 4. show (Type::Float)\nThe printer. JS uses `String(number)` which prints:\n- finite: shortest round-trip decimal (e.g. \"2\", \"5\", \"1.5\", \"0.5\")\n- NaN -> \"NaN\"\n- Infinity -> \"Infinity\"\n- -Infinity -> \"-Infinity\"\n\nWait, but \"1.5 + 0.25 * 2.0\" prints \"2\" in JS. And \"2.5 * 2.0\" prints \"5\". So finite floats that are integral print without a decimal point. That's the shortest round-trip.\n\nIn Rust, `f64`'s `Display` also prints the shortest round-trip: `1.5.to_string()` = \"1.5\", `2.0.to_string()` = \"2\". Actually Rust's `{}` for f64 uses a \"shortest round-trip\" representation since Rust 1.0 via Grisu/Ryū. `2.0f64.to_string()` == \"2\". Good. But non-finite:\n- NaN -> \"NaN\" ✓\n- +inf -> \"inf\" ✗ (JS: \"Infinity\")\n- -inf -> \"-inf\" ✗ (JS: \"-Infinity\")\n\nSo the Rust backend needs a custom show for Float to convert \"inf\"/\"-inf\" to \"Infinity\"/\"-Infinity\". NaN already matches.\n\nActually, the requirement from the issue: \"NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\". The JS backend prints \"Infinity\", \"-Infinity\", \"NaN\". So the Rust backend must match that.\n\nLet me write a helper function in emit_rs.rs or use float.rs. Since the JS reference uses `crate::float::lit` for literals only (finite). For printing, JS does it inline via `String(value)`. For Rust, I could write:\n\n```rust\nType::Float => {\n    // f64's Display is the shortest round-trip spelling, matching JS's String(); only the\n    // non-finite names differ: inf/-inf become Infinity/-Infinity to agree byte for byte.\n    format!(\"({value}.to_string().replace(\\\"inf\\\", \\\"Infinity\\\"))\")\n}\n```\n\nWait, but `-inf` -> replace(\"inf\", \"Infinity\") gives \"-Infinity\". And `+inf` -> \"Infinity\". But careful: could a finite value's string contain \"inf\"? No, finite decimal strings never contain \"inf\". So a simple replace works. But cleaner to write a helper. Actually, let me think about what's cleanest and most maintainable. \n\nHmm, but `value` here is a Rust expression string like `t_0` or `e1.clone()`. So I can't compute at emit time. It's emitted code that runs at runtime. So the emit needs to produce Rust code that formats an f64 and fixes the names.\n\nOptions:\n- Inline: `format!(\"({value}.to_string().replace(\\\"inf\\\", \\\"Infinity\\\"))\")` — but replace on \"inf\" substring. For a positive infinity, `f64::INFINITY.to_string()` is \"inf\", replace -> \"Infinity\". For NaN, \"NaN\" unaffected. For -inf, \"-inf\" -> \"-Infinity\". Good.\n- A helper `tl_fmt_f64(v: f64) -> String` in the emitted preamble.\n\nSince the project prefers minimal helper additions and the emit_rs already has lots of helpers... Actually a dedicated helper is cleaner and matches the JS pattern where `String(value)` handles everything. But wait — there's also the concern that `value.to_string()` for floats might need the value by reference or by value. In show, value is a string expression. For `Type::Int | Type::Int64 => format!(\"({value}).to_string()\")`. For Float, value might be owned (like `t_0`) or a cloned reference (like `e0.clone()`). `(e0.clone()).to_string()` works.\n\nLet me consider: does Rust's f64 Display actually match JS's Number-to-string for all finite values? Both use shortest round-trip (Grisu/Ryū). JS Number.prototype.toString uses shortest round-trip that round-trips. Rust's Display for f64 also uses shortest round-trip. They should agree for all values, except possibly edge cases. The JS commit message says \"JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\" and the float.rs comment says \"Rust's Display for f64 is the shortest decimal that round-trips to the same double -- exactly what a literal needs to be exact on every target, and what JS's own String(number) produces for the same value.\" So the repo has already established that Rust's Display and JS's String agree. \n\nSo the only difference is inf/-inf naming. I'll write a helper:\n\n```rust\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_show_f64(v: f64) -> String {\n    match v {\n        f64::INFINITY => \"Infinity\".to_string(),\n        f64::NEG_INFINITY => \"-Infinity\".to_string(),\n        _ => v.to_string(),\n    }\n}\n\"#;\n```\n\nAnd in show: `Type::Float => format!(\"tl_show_f64({value})\")`.\n\nHmm wait, but there's `is_nan` too. NaN's Display is \"NaN\" already, matches JS. So no special case needed for NaN.\n\nNow, how to decide when to include the helper? The `emit` function uses `uses(...)` string detection. I can check `uses(\"tl_show_f64(\")`. But careful — `show` may be called for Float even when the program's output type contains a Float. Also jsonlines printing, etc. The `uses` detection is on the final decls string. Fine.\n\n### 5. arith\nCurrent:\n```rust\nfn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Div if *ty == Type::Int64 => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Rem if *ty == Type::Int64 => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l}).wrapping_add({r})\"),\n        BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l}).wrapping_sub({r})\"),\n        BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l}).wrapping_mul({r})\"),\n        other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n    }\n}\n```\n\nFor Float: `f64` division by zero returns Infinity (IEEE). `f64` has no wrapping_* (those are integer methods). So I need a Float arm:\n\n```rust\nfn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n    if *ty == Type::Float {\n        // f64 arithmetic is the IEEE binary64 the type already is (ADR 0007), including\n        // division by zero answering Infinity rather than trapping.\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"({l} / {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else {\n        match op {\n            ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, but division by zero on f64 in Rust: `1.0f64 / 0.0f64` — is this defined? In Rust, floating point division by zero is defined as IEEE: `1.0 / 0.0` == INFINITY. Yes, it doesn't panic. Good. Actually wait, let me double check — in Rust, `f64` division by zero produces Infinity, no panic (unlike integer division which panics in debug). Yes.\n\nBut hold on — the checker: for Int arithmetic, the checker guards against division by zero at runtime via tl_div. For Float, division by zero is total (returns Infinity), so no guard. Good.\n\n### 6. input parser (tl_parse_f64)\nThe Rust backend has a hand-written JSON parser. I need a function that reads a JSON number into f64. JSON numbers: `-?(0|[1-9]\\d*)(\\.\\d+)?([eE][+-]?\\d+)?`. The existing `tl_parse_i32` reads a sign + digits, then refuses `.` or `e`. I need `tl_parse_f64` to also consume `.` and exponent and parse via `str::parse::<f64>()`.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```rust\nfn tl_parse_f64(p: &mut TlParser) -> f64 {\n    p.skip_ws();\n    let start = p.p;\n    if p.p < p.b.len() && matches!(p.b[p.p], b'-' | b'+') {\n        p.p += 1;\n    }\n    while p.p < p.b.len() && p.b[p.p].is_ascii_digit() {\n        p.p += 1;\n    }\n    if p.p == start {\n        tl_fail(\"expected a number\");\n    }\n    if p.p < p.b.len() && p.b[p.p] == b'.' {\n        p.p += 1;\n        if p.p >= p.b.len() || !p.b[p.p].is_ascii_digit() {\n            tl_fail(\"expected a digit after the decimal point\");\n        }\n        while p.p < p.b.len() && p.b[p.p].is_ascii_digit() {\n            p.p += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    if p.p < p.b.len() && matches!(p.b[p.p], b'e' | b'E') {\n        p.p += 1;\n        if p.p < p.b.len() && matches!(p.b[p.p], b'-' | b'+') {\n            p.p += 1;\n        }\n        if p.p >= p.b.len() || !p.b[p.p].is_ascii_digit() {\n            tl_fail(\"expected a digit in the exponent\");\n        }\n        while p.p < p.b.len() && p.b[p.p].is_ascii_digit() {\n            p.p += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    let text = std::str::from_utf8(&p.b[start..p.p]).expect(\"digits and signs are ascii\");\n    text.parse().unwrap_or_else(|_| tl_fail(\"number is out of range\"))\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — the existing `tl_parse_i32` checks `if p.p == start` and then errors if next is `.`/`e`/`E`. For f64, I need to consume those. But there's a subtlety: what if it's `1.5`? start=0, p advances past `1`, then `.`, etc. text = \"1.5\", parse fine.\n\nBut wait — there's the trailing content check. After parsing a value, `tl_read_value` checks `p.p != p.b.len()` -> \"trailing content after the value\". And `tl_parse_line` also checks trailing. Good.\n\nAlso there's a question of whether the JSON number like `-0` parses fine. f64 parse of \"-0\" is -0.0. Fine.\n\nAlso, should I reuse a generic approach? The existing code has separate functions per scalar type. Adding `tl_parse_f64` matches the pattern.\n\nHmm, but one consideration: the PARSER_HELPER is only included when `reads_value || uses(\"TlParser\")`. `tl_parse_f64` is inside PARSER_HELPER (it's part of that const string). Let me verify: yes, PARSER_HELPER contains TlParser and the tl_parse_* functions. So adding tl_parse_f64 to PARSER_HELPER means it's always emitted with the parser. Good.\n\n### Checker: does the checker allow Float in input types?\nThe JS commit changed input.rs to allow `(Type::Float, Value::Number(_))`. But did it change the checker to allow Float in input type declarations? Let me check check/mod.rs for how Int64 is refused and whether Float is allowed. The commit's check/mod.rs diff didn't touch input type validation. Let me search the current check/mod.rs for Int64 input refusal.\n\nLet me grep for \"Int64\" in check/mod.rs and see how input types are validated.\n\n### Also, emit_toylang.rs already has Float literal printing via float::lit. That's shared, done.\n\n### build.rs\nAlready has ToRust for f64 and Type::Float. Done.\n\n### tags.rs\nDone.\n\n### Now the corpus cases\nThe JS commit added backend_js.rs tests (JS-only) and no corpus cases. Wait, actually let me check if there are corpus cases with float. The commit diff for tests didn't include corpus. Let me check the current corpus for any float cases. Actually, since corpus cases run on every backend, and only JS and (after my change) Rust support Float, corpus cases would fail on the other backends. So no corpus cases yet. The JS commit's tests are in tests/backend_js.rs, JS-only. I should add analogous tests in tests/backend_rs.rs, Rust-only.\n\nLet me look at tests/backend_rs.rs to see its structure.\n\nAlso, the task says: \"while iterating, test with `just check`\". `just check` is the fast loop. Let me understand what `just check` runs. Let me read the justfile.\n\nLet me also check: does `just check` run the tests including backend_rs.rs? Let me look.\n\nLet me now read the relevant files:\n- justfile\n- tests/backend_rs.rs\n- tests/backend_js.rs (already seen the float tests)\n- the current emit_rs.rs in full (I have most of it)\n- check/mod.rs for input validation of Int64/Float\n- src/parse.rs for float grammar (already landed, so I don't need to change it)\n\nLet me start reading.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063230677001hOokfnm8SZhD2L","sessionID":"ses_f9cdf1744ffeXefkzNuE7Vt14g","messageID":"msg_06321fbfc001maX3XGlSsDn1Qo"}}
